WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.090
Jon Bentley once asked professional

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.990
programmers to write binary search,

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.530
about a dozen lines of code.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.260
Around ninety percent of

5
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.800
their programs had bugs.

6
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.090
Most of them broke at the edges.

7
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.100
Today: testing like an engineer.

8
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.440
A unit test runs one small piece of

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.960
code and checks the answer.

10
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.150
In Python, assert stops the

11
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.070
program if its check is false.

12
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.950
This function passes the first test.

13
00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.650
But the second fails: best

14
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.870
starts at zero, which is bigger

15
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.510
than every negative number.

16
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.790
Ordinary data rarely breaks

17
00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.790
code; the edges do.

18
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.300
Test an empty list and a list of one.

19
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.120
Test the first and the last position.

20
00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.050
Test values that repeat, negatives, and

21
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.810
something that isn’t there at all.

22
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.810
Here’s the outline of a binary

23
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.790
search that passes a test in the

24
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.990
middle of a long list.

25
00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.270
But search a one-item list for its only

26
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.590
item, and it says minus one: not found.

27
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.000
The loop must run while lo is less

28
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.720
than or equal to hi, or it skips

29
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.880
the last place to look.

30
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.100
Four short tests catch it.

31
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.760
An empty list, one item, the last

32
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.260
item, and a value that isn’t there.

33
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.250
Run them after every change, and a

34
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.970
boundary bug can’t sneak back in.

35
00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:33.610
Quick check!

36
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.810
A function finds the

37
00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.230
largest mark in a list.

38
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.990
Which test is most likely to catch a bug?

39
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.690
The list of only negative

40
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.250
numbers: an edge case.

41
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.160
Five ordinary marks would pass

42
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.600
even the buggy version.

43
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.640
Write the tests that try to break your

44
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.660
code, not the ones that flatter it.

45
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.240
Engineers often write the test first.

46
00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.730
Red: write a test, and watch it fail.

47
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.440
Green: write the simplest

48
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.520
code that passes.

49
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.820
Refactor: tidy the code while

50
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.280
the tests still pass.

51
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.930
Then the tests are a safety net

52
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.350
for every later change.

53
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.350
Tests can’t catch everything.

54
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.770
A second pair of eyes spots

55
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.170
unclear names and missing cases.

56
00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.730
Keep each change small, say why you

57
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.630
made it, and review kindly: comment

58
00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.590
on the code, not the coder.

59
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.050
In the app, open Guess the number.

60
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.300
Find a hidden number from one to a

61
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.060
thousand by halving, and count your

62
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:40.320
guesses.

63
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.820
Then test the edges: can halving

64
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.100
find one, and a thousand?

65
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:47.990
Your turn.

66
00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.490
You’re working test-first

67
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.790
on a new function.

68
00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.170
What do you write before the code?

69
00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.540
A test that fails.

70
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.650
In short: unit tests check

71
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.690
small pieces with assert.

72
00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.610
Test the edges: empty, one item,

73
00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.850
first and last, not there.

74
00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.610
Work test-first: red, green, refactor.

75
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.560
And review code with others.

76
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.600
Try it on your own code: take a function

77
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.720
you wrote this term, and write five

78
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.380
asserts for it, at least three at the

79
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:24.560
edges.

80
00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:25.850
Run them.

81
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.740
If one fails, you’ve found a bug

82
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.220
before anyone else did.
